[cot:thought] I am writing a comprehensive data module for the adaptation-economics concentration post.
[cot:thought] The core narrative
[cot:thought] centers on extreme concentration of residual climate-damage burden and adaptation finance.
[cot:thought] Top-1 households carry 40% of residual costs. Top-3 households carry roughly 87%. N.America, South Asia, and Latin America lead regional protection gaps. OECD adaptation finance is heavily donor-concentrated.
[cot:thought] Loans dominate instruments at 67%. Adaptation spending is only 3% of global climate finance.
[cot:thought] A HHI calculation for residual costs reaches over 2800. N.America carries
[cot:thought] 22.4% of the $424 billion protection gap. South Asia, Latin America, Sub-Saharan Africa, and Europe follow at roughly 15.6%, 14.6%, 13.7%, and 13.0%. The top three regions hold about 53% of the total gap. OECD adaptation donors are similarly lopsided: Germany leads at 18%, followed by Japan at 16%, France at 12%, the United States at 11%, the United Kingdom at 8%, the
[cot:thought] Netherlands at 6%, and the rest of the world at 29%. The headline metric for the post will be the top-one-to-top-three share.
